---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";



const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Can a Generator Power a Furnace, Fridge and Sump Pump?", url: "/can-a-generator-power-a-furnace-refrigerator-and-sump-pump/" },
    { title: "Can a Generator Power an Electric Water Heater?", url: "/can-a-portable-generator-power-an-electric-water-heater/" },
    { title: "Generator Transfer Switch Guide", url: "/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "Generac Generator Cost in 2026";
const description =
  "What a Generac generator actually costs in 2026 — unit prices by kW, transfer switch, gas line, permits, and the installed total homeowners really pay.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Cost Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Generac Generator Cost",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/generac-generator-cost/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a Generac whole house generator cost installed?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a typical home, a Generac Guardian 18-22kW whole house generator costs $9,000-13,000 installed in 2026, with the generator itself accounting for roughly $4,600-5,600 of that. Smaller 10-14kW units run $6,500-9,500 installed. The balance covers the automatic transfer switch, electrical labor, gas line work, pad, and permits.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is Generac installation so expensive?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A standby generator install involves three trades: an electrician to wire the automatic transfer switch into your service panel, a gas fitter to run and often upsize the fuel supply, and site work for the pad. Add permits and inspections and installation typically costs as much as the generator itself. Gas meter upgrades are the most common cost surprise, adding $1,500-3,000.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a Generac 22kW generator cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Generac Guardian 22kW unit alone costs roughly $5,000-5,600 in 2026. Installed, most homeowners pay $9,000-13,000, with a median around $11,200 in the quotes we tracked. It is the most commonly installed residential size because it covers a typical whole home including central air conditioning.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a Generac generator worth the cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on your outage risk. If your area loses power for hours a few times a year, a portable generator plus a manual transfer switch covers you for around $2,000. If outages routinely last days, someone in the home depends on powered medical equipment, or you travel and cannot be home to start a portable, the automatic whole-home coverage is difficult to replicate any other way.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does it cost to run a Generac generator per day?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 22kW Guardian running on natural gas costs roughly $3-5 per hour, or about $70-120 for a full day of continuous outage at 2026 residential rates. On propane, the same unit costs closer to $6-9 per hour because propane delivers less energy per dollar in most markets.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size Generac generator do I need for a 2,000 square foot house?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a 2,000 square foot home with central air conditioning, an 18kW or 22kW Guardian is the standard recommendation. A 14kW unit can work if you are willing to use load management modules to shed the air conditioner during peak demand, which is often cheaper than stepping up a generator size.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a Generac generator increase home value?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Real estate industry estimates commonly put the added value at 3-5% of home value in outage-prone regions, though appraisers treat it inconsistently and it rarely returns the full installed cost. It is more reliably a selling-speed advantage than a dollar-for-dollar investment return.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "generac generator cost",
      "generac standby generator price",
      "whole house generator cost",
      "generator installation cost",
      "home standby generator",
      "home improvement"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Cost Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          How Much Does a <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Generac Generator</span> Cost in 2026?
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We priced Generac units across every size, then added the parts nobody quotes upfront — transfer switch, gas line, concrete pad, permits, and labor. Here is the real installed number.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#installed-cost-table"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Installed Prices
          </a>
          <a
            href="#sizing"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            What Size Do I Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="21.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Cost at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#unit-prices" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Unit Prices by Model</a>
          <a href="#installed-cost-table" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Installation Costs</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing Your Unit</a>
          <a href="#portable-vs-standby" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Portable vs Standby</a>
          <a href="#running-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Running Costs</a>
          <a href="#save-money" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Save Money</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The sticker price on a Generac standby generator is the smallest part of the conversation. A 22kW Guardian unit that lists around $5,200 routinely lands between $9,000 and $13,000 by the time it is bolted to a pad, wired to an automatic transfer switch, tied into a gas line, and signed off by an inspector. Here is exactly where every dollar goes — unit, install, and the long-term running cost most quotes never mention.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Generac Generator Cost at a Glance -->
        <div id="quick-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Generac Generator Cost at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Across the quotes and dealer pricing we tracked in 2026, <strong>Generac generator cost</strong> falls into three clear tiers. Portable units are a hardware purchase you can wheel home the same day. Whole-home standby units are a construction project with a generator attached. The gap between those two realities is where most homeowners get surprised.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Category</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Unit Only</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Installed Total</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Covers</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Portable (3-8kW)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$450-1,400</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$450-2,900</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials via cords or a manual switch</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PWRcell Battery</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$10,000-16,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$15,000-25,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Selected circuits, silent, solar-pairable</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 10-14kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,800-4,200</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,500-9,500</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essential circuits, one AC zone</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 18-22kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,600-5,600</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$9,000-13,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most whole homes, central AC included</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protector 26-48kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$9,000-18,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$16,000-30,000+</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large homes, shops, light commercial</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$11,200</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Median installed 22kW quote we tracked</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">55%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Share of that total that is not the generator</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1,100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical automatic transfer switch, installed</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">$300/yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Average annual maintenance contract</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Generac Unit Prices by Model and Size -->
        <div id="unit-prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Generac Unit Prices by Model and Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are hardware-only prices — the generator on a pallet, before an electrician touches it. Generac sells through dealers rather than at a fixed MSRP, so regional variation of 10-15% is normal, and dealers frequently bundle the unit and install into a single number rather than itemizing.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Model</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Output</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Unit Price</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">GP3500iO Inverter</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000W running</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-800</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">GP6500 Portable</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6,500W running</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$750-950</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">GP8000E Portable</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8,000W running</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,000-1,300</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 10kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10kW standby</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,800-3,400</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 14kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14kW standby</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3,700-4,200</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 18kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18kW standby</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,600-5,100</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 22kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22kW standby</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5,000-5,600</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 26kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26kW standby</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,800-7,800</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protector 38-48kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">38-48kW liquid-cooled</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$13,000-18,000</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pricing curve is not linear, and that matters when you are choosing between two adjacent sizes. Stepping from 18kW to 22kW adds roughly $500 to the unit price for 22% more output — one of the best value jumps in the lineup, and the reason 22kW is the most commonly installed residential size. Stepping from 22kW to 26kW adds closer to $2,000, because 26kW uses a larger engine and enclosure. Above that, the Protector series moves to liquid cooling, and prices roughly double.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: What Installation Actually Adds -->
        <div id="installed-cost-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Installation Actually Adds
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation is where the <strong>Generac generator cost</strong> conversation gets real. A standby generator needs a level pad, a fuel supply sized to the engine's demand, a transfer switch wired into your service panel, and a permit with an inspection. Each of those is a separate trade or line item.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Line Item</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Cost</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Automatic transfer switch</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$700-1,800</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whole-home 200A switches sit at the top</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electrical labor</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,200-3,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rises with distance from the panel</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas line run and upsizing</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$800-2,500</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A 22kW unit often forces a meter upgrade</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete or composite pad</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$250-900</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Composite pads are cheaper and code-legal in most areas</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Permits and inspection</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$150-800</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrical and gas permits are usually separate</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Propane tank (if no natural gas)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500-3,500</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leasing instead of buying cuts upfront cost</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Delivery, crane, or trenching</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0-1,500</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tight or sloped lots drive this up fast</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Add those together and the pattern is consistent: <strong>installation typically costs as much as the generator itself, sometimes more</strong>. On the 22kW quotes we reviewed, the unit averaged roughly 45% of the total and everything else made up the balance. If a quote comes in dramatically below that range, ask specifically whether the transfer switch, gas work, and permit fees are included or listed as allowances.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Line Item That Blows Up Budgets</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Gas supply. A 22kW Guardian consumes roughly 300 cubic feet of natural gas per hour at full load, and many older homes have a meter and service line sized for a furnace, water heater, and range — not that on top. When the utility has to upsize the meter or the service run, that single item can add $1,500-3,000 and weeks of scheduling delay. Ask your installer to confirm gas capacity before you sign anything.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: What Size Generac Do You Actually Need? -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Generac Do You Actually Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oversizing is the most expensive mistake in this category, and it is easy to make because dealers are rarely penalized for selling you more generator than you need. Sizing correctly can save thousands both upfront and in fuel over the unit's life.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">10-14kW: Essential Circuits</h3>
          <p>
            Enough for a refrigerator, furnace blower, sump pump, well pump, lighting circuits, internet, and device charging, plus one modest air conditioning zone. This is the right size for a smaller home, a mild climate, or a household that genuinely only wants the lights and the fridge to stay on. Installed, expect $6,500-9,500.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">18-22kW: Most Whole Homes</h3>
          <p>
            The sweet spot for a typical 2,000-3,000 square foot home with central air, an electric range or dryer, and a well pump. A 22kW unit handles central AC starting surge alongside the rest of the house without load-shedding, which is why it is the volume seller. Installed, expect $9,000-13,000.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">26kW and Up: Large or All-Electric Homes</h3>
          <p>
            Needed when you have two AC compressors, an electric water heater plus electric range, a heat pump, an EV charger you want live during an outage, or more than about 4,000 square feet. Above 26kW you move into the liquid-cooled Protector line, and installed costs climb past $16,000.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Load-Shedding Alternative</h3>
          <p>
            Generac's smart management modules let a smaller generator serve a larger house by temporarily dropping non-critical loads — typically an AC compressor or electric water heater — when demand spikes. Two modules cost a few hundred dollars and can let an 18kW unit do the job of a 22kW installation. If your quote assumes the larger unit, ask whether load management would let you step down a size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Generac Portable vs Standby: The Real Cost Comparison -->
        <div id="portable-vs-standby" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Generac Portable vs Standby: The Real Cost Comparison
          </h2>

          <p>
            A Generac GP8000E portable and a Guardian 22kW both keep your food cold during a storm. The difference is roughly $10,000, and whether you have to go outside in the rain to make it happen.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Portable</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Standby</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Upfront total</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$450-2,900</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,500-13,000</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Starts during outage</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">You start it, manually</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Automatic, under 30 seconds</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Refueling</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 8-12 hours</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None on natural gas</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Central AC</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely, only the largest units</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes at 18kW+</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Resale value added</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly cited at 3-5% of home value</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The honest decision rule: if outages in your area last hours and happen a few times a year, a portable plus a manual transfer switch — roughly $2,000 all in — covers you sensibly. If outages routinely run into days, if anyone in the house depends on powered medical equipment, or if you travel and cannot be home to start a portable, the standby premium buys something a portable genuinely cannot provide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Fuel, Maintenance, and Long-Term Running Cost -->
        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel, Maintenance, and Long-Term Running Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase is one number. Owning a Generac for a decade is another, and it is small relative to the install but not zero.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel Consumption</h3>
          <p>
            A 22kW Guardian burns roughly 200 cubic feet of natural gas per hour at half load and around 300 at full load. At typical 2026 residential gas rates, that works out to about $3-5 per hour of runtime, or $70-120 for a full day of outage. On propane, the same unit consumes roughly 2.1 gallons per hour at half load — meaningfully more expensive, commonly $6-9 per hour depending on your propane contract.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance</h3>
          <p>
            Generac specifies an annual service, or every 100-200 run hours, whichever comes first: oil and filter, air filter, spark plugs, and a battery check. A dealer maintenance contract runs $200-450 per year. Doing it yourself with a Generac maintenance kit costs $60-120 in parts and about an hour of work, and does not void the warranty on residential units as long as you keep records.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Battery</h3>
          <p>
            The single most common cause of a standby generator failing to start is a dead starting battery — the same failure mode as a car. Budget $80-150 every three to four years, and treat a battery warning on the controller or the mobile app as urgent rather than routine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ten-Year Cost of Ownership</h3>
          <p>
            For a typical 22kW installation: roughly $11,000 installed, plus $3,000 in maintenance, plus two batteries, plus fuel for perhaps 60 hours of outages per decade in an average area. That lands around $15,000 over ten years, or about $125 per month of insurance against losing power. Whether that is worth it depends almost entirely on how often your area actually goes dark.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: How to Lower Your Generac Quote -->
        <div id="save-money" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Lower Your Generac Quote
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is real room to negotiate on a standby generator install, more than most homeowners assume. These are the levers that actually moved numbers in the quotes we compared.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Get Three Quotes, Itemized</h3>
          <p>
            Spreads of $3,000-4,000 on identical 22kW installations were common in our comparisons. Insist that each quote break out unit, transfer switch, electrical labor, gas work, pad, and permits separately — bundled single-number quotes make it impossible to see where one installer is padding.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy in the Off-Season</h3>
          <p>
            Demand spikes after every major regional storm, and pricing follows. Late winter and early spring are consistently the cheapest windows, with shorter lead times and dealers more willing to discount to fill their schedule.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shorten the Runs</h3>
          <p>
            Every foot between the generator, the meter, and the electrical panel adds conduit, wire, trenching, and labor. Siting the unit close to both — while respecting Generac's clearance requirements from windows, doors, and vents — is the single largest controllable cost in the install.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size Honestly, Then Add Load Management</h3>
          <p>
            Do the wattage math on what you genuinely need running. If the gap between two sizes is one air conditioner, smart management modules are dramatically cheaper than stepping up an entire generator size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Financing and Utility Programs Before Paying Cash</h3>
          <p>
            Generac and its dealers routinely run promotional financing, and some utilities and states offer rebates or resilience incentives, particularly for households with medical power dependencies. These change frequently, so ask your dealer and check your utility's current program list rather than assuming none exist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a Generac whole house generator cost installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical home, a Generac Guardian 18-22kW whole house generator costs $9,000-13,000 installed in 2026, with the generator itself accounting for roughly $4,600-5,600 of that. Smaller 10-14kW units run $6,500-9,500 installed. The balance covers the automatic transfer switch, electrical labor, gas line work, pad, and permits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is Generac installation so expensive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standby generator install involves three trades: an electrician to wire the automatic transfer switch into your service panel, a gas fitter to run and often upsize the fuel supply, and site work for the pad. Add permits and inspections and installation typically costs as much as the generator itself. Gas meter upgrades are the most common cost surprise, adding $1,500-3,000.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a Generac 22kW generator cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Generac Guardian 22kW unit alone costs roughly $5,000-5,600 in 2026. Installed, most homeowners pay $9,000-13,000, with a median around $11,200 in the quotes we tracked. It is the most commonly installed residential size because it covers a typical whole home including central air conditioning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Generac generator worth the cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your outage risk. If your area loses power for hours a few times a year, a portable generator plus a manual transfer switch covers you for around $2,000. If outages routinely last days, someone in the home depends on powered medical equipment, or you travel and cannot be home to start a portable, the automatic whole-home coverage is difficult to replicate any other way.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a Generac generator per day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 22kW Guardian running on natural gas costs roughly $3-5 per hour, or about $70-120 for a full day of continuous outage at 2026 residential rates. On propane, the same unit costs closer to $6-9 per hour because propane delivers less energy per dollar in most markets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size Generac generator do I need for a 2,000 square foot house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a 2,000 square foot home with central air conditioning, an 18kW or 22kW Guardian is the standard recommendation. A 14kW unit can work if you are willing to use load management modules to shed the air conditioner during peak demand, which is often cheaper than stepping up a generator size.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a Generac generator increase home value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Real estate industry estimates commonly put the added value at 3-5% of home value in outage-prone regions, though appraisers treat it inconsistently and it rarely returns the full installed cost. It is more reliably a selling-speed advantage than a dollar-for-dollar investment return.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Costly Mistakes When Buying a Generac
          </h2>

          <p>
            The expensive errors in this category almost never involve the generator itself. They involve everything around it.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Accepting a Single Bundled Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A quote that reads "22kW Generac, installed, $13,500" hides where the money goes. Without itemized transfer switch, labor, gas, pad, and permit lines, you cannot compare bids or spot padding. Every reputable installer will itemize on request; treat refusal as a signal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Confirming Gas Capacity First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Homeowners routinely sign a contract before anyone checks whether the gas meter and service line can feed a 22kW engine on top of the furnace and water heater. When the utility has to upsize, it adds thousands and weeks. Make gas capacity verification a condition before you commit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying More Generator Than the House Needs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stepping from 18kW to 26kW because a salesperson called it future-proofing can add $4,000-6,000 to the installed total for capacity that never gets used. Do the load math, then ask whether load management modules close the gap for a few hundred dollars instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Siting the Unit for Looks Instead of Cost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Putting the generator on the far side of the house to hide it from the street means longer conduit, longer gas runs, more trenching, and more labor — often $1,000-2,500 in avoidable cost. Balance appearance against the real price of distance from the panel and meter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Maintenance Until It Fails to Start</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standby generator that has not had an oil change or a battery check in four years is a very expensive lawn ornament during a storm. Dead batteries are the number one cause of no-start failures. Either buy the service contract or do the annual kit yourself, but do not do nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

